WEBVTT

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:09.770
<v Ira Glass>We're supposed to smell the roses, notice the sunsets, live life to the fullest. But this is not always possible. In fact, it's not always advisable.
<v Ira Glass>我们理应去闻闻玫瑰花香，看看落日余晖，尽情享受生活。但这并非总是可能的。事实上，这也并非总是明智的。

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:17.640
<v Ira Glass>Robert says that he began the part of his life when he felt the most alive a few years ago, when he was on a trip to Ireland. He attended a horse jumping competition in a big stadium.
<v Ira Glass>罗伯特说，他感到人生最鲜活的阶段始于几年前的一次爱尔兰之旅。当时他去参加了一个大型体育场举办的马术跳跃比赛。

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:29.280
<v Robert Lundin>It was a great international competition. And they had Ireland, of course, was there. And Britain and France and Italy and Germany, and Australia, I think, was there.
<v Robert Lundin>那是一场伟大的国际赛事。当然，爱尔兰队参赛了。还有英国、法国、意大利、德国，我想澳大利亚也参加了。

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:59.160
<v Robert Lundin>Well, I was watching that. And I was thinking, "Well, God, what do you want me to do with my life?" And so I was at the International Jumping Derby, and I noticed that the United States wasn't there. And so I took this as a message from above that I should do something to improve the riding program in the United States.
<v Robert Lundin>那时我正在观看比赛，心想：“天哪，你究竟想让我的人生去做什么呢？”当时我身处国际跳跃德比大赛现场，我注意到美国队并没有参赛。于是，我将此视为上天的旨意，认为我应该做点什么来改善美国的马术项目。

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:18.610
<v Robert Lundin>I felt that I was struck by a bolt of lightning of sorts, a kind of a psychic bolt of lightning. I said, "This is what God wants me to do." And so that was my mission, was to improve the riding program of the United States.
<v Robert Lundin>我感觉自己被某种闪电击中了，一种精神上的闪电。我说：“这就是上帝要我做的事。”因此，改善美国马术项目成了我的使命。

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:37.270
<v Ira Glass>At that time, Robert had already suffered from a few bouts of psychosis and delusion. One time, he thought a light bulb in his closet was giving him special messages from God. Another time, he believed he was an angel with special powers, sent to help mankind. But saving the US equestrian team, there was something so earthly about that, so modest.
<v Ira Glass>那时候，罗伯特已经经历过几次精神病和妄想症的发作。有一次，他认为衣橱里的灯泡在向他传递上帝的特殊信息。另一次，他坚信自己是派遣来拯救人类的拥有特殊力量的天使。但拯救美国马术队这件事，却显得如此世俗，如此谦卑。

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:47.220
<v Ira Glass>Robert loved horses, loved to ride. And one thing led to another. And before long, he was the nation's number one salesman of US equestrian team t-shirts and caps and paraphernalia.
<v Ira Glass>罗伯特热爱马匹，热爱骑行。事情一件接着一件地发展。没过多久，他就成了美国马术队的T恤、帽子和相关装备的全国头号推销员。

00:01:47.220 --> 00:02:09.160
<v Ira Glass>He would get the stuff from a wholesaler, go to horse shows, sell it to horsey enthusiasts, trying to drum up support for the cause, all without any official sanction or authorization of the US equestrian team. It was exciting. He traveled all over the country, met people, talked about his mission. At one point, he became convinced that the way to save the US equestrian team would be to involve the US military.
<v Ira Glass>他会从批发商那里进货，去马展上把东西卖给马术爱好者，试图为这项事业争取支持，而这一切都没有得到美国马术队的正式许可或授权。这很令人兴奋。他跑遍了全国，结识了许多人，讲述着他的使命。在某一时刻，他开始确信拯救美国马术队的方法就是让美国军方参与进来。

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:33.230
<v Robert Lundin>Because this is who rode-- when I went to the jumping festival in Ireland, the military, or a military program, represented Ireland, and they won. And so I thought the United States should do the same thing, that we should have military riders and military equestrians represent the United States.
<v Robert Lundin>因为当我在爱尔兰参加跳跃节时，是军方代表爱尔兰参赛的，而且他们赢了。所以我认为美国也应该这样做，我们应该让军事骑手和军事马术选手代表美国。

00:02:33.230 --> 00:03:10.380
<v Robert Lundin>So I decided that I would write a letter to Senator Sam Nunn, who was very familiar with defense activity. And I wrote him a letter saying as politely as I could, do you think that the US Army could come up with an equestrian team. And actually, he went off, and his staff researched it for me. And they came back. And I got a letter from somebody in the Pentagon. And they said, "No, I'm sorry. We can't include equestrian in our sports program. We don't have the resources."
<v Robert Lundin>所以我决定写信给非常熟悉国防事务的萨姆·纳恩参议员。我在信中尽可能礼貌地询问，您认为美国陆军能组建一支马术队吗？实际上，他确实去做了，他的幕僚为我进行了研究，最后他们回复了我。我收到了五角大楼某人的回信。他们说：“不，很抱歉。我们的体育项目中无法包含马术。我们没有那样的资源。”

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:20.880
<v Ira Glass>After a few years, Robert's mission started to fall apart. He was losing money. The people running the US equestrian team let him know they didn't actually want him to save them. So he stopped.
<v Ira Glass>几年后，罗伯特的使命开始破灭。他一直在亏钱。经营美国马术队的人也让他知道，他们并不真的需要他的拯救。于是他停止了。

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:42.690
<v Ira Glass>And when he looks back on those times these days, he said it was like he was too alive back then, too alive. He says back then, he had a sickness that made him believe every idea with complete conviction, that made him act on every inspired notion that entered his head. Now, he works a normal job, sleeps normal hours, commutes a normal commute, takes medications which protect him from fits of mania.
<v Ira Glass>如今当他回首往事时，他说那时的他感觉太“鲜活”了，太鲜活了。他说那时他患有一种疾病，使他能以绝对的信念相信每一个想法，让他对脑海中出现的每一个灵感都付诸行动。现在，他做着普通的工作，睡着正常的觉，通勤着正常的路线，服用药物来保护自己免受狂躁发作的影响。

00:03:42.690 --> 00:04:02.370
<v Robert Lundin>I think I was a lot more creative when I was slightly manic. In a way, I was certainly a little bit wilder and maybe a little bit more fun.
<v Robert Lundin>我觉得我在轻度狂躁时更有创造力。在某种程度上，我当然更野一点，也可能更有趣一点。

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:10.740
<v Ira Glass>In fact, when he and acquaintances who suffered through similar kinds of mental illness get together, sometimes they'll all reminisce about the old days, before they were medicated.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，当他和经历过类似精神疾病的熟人聚在一起时，有时他们会一起怀念用药之前的那些旧时光。

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:32.160
<v Robert Lundin>Oh, yeah. We all talk about how many times we've been arrested or something like that. That's conversation. We talk about the goofy things we did. "I got arrested. I remember that. The police was very nice. But I broke a vase, and he took me to the hospital." And we talk about our hospitalizations.
<v Robert Lundin>噢，是的。我们都会聊起我们被捕过多少次之类的话题。这就是我们的谈话内容。我们谈论我们做过的傻事。“我被捕了。我记得那次。警察很友善。但我打碎了一个花瓶，他把我送到了医院。”我们还会聊起住院的经历。

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:46.100
<v Robert Lundin>And I do reminisce a little bit about it. It was fun. We had a lot of fun and went all over the country and had friends. And it was kind of exciting, sure.
<v Robert Lundin>我也确实会偶尔怀念那段日子。那时很开心。我们有很多乐趣，跑遍了全国，交了很多朋友。那确实挺令人兴奋的，当然。

00:04:46.100 --> 00:05:08.330
<v Robert Lundin>But now I'm socked in a schedule of being responsible and going to work every day. And these things are not fun compared to running around the country and going to horse shows. Those were more fun. But this is what I've chosen to do.
<v Robert Lundin>但现在我被束缚在负责任和每天上班的日程表里。比起跑遍全国去参加马展，这些事情一点也不好玩。那时候确实更有趣。但这是我选择的生活方式。

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:42.040
<v Ira Glass>It doesn't just happen among people who are medicated, the desire to avoid the highs and lows of a frantic life. Many people choose a steadier life, a duller life, because it's also a saner life, or because it's all they can manage. Today on our program, Avoiding the High Cost of Living, people stepping back from one big life experience or another, and why that may be the only possible choice sometimes. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>不仅仅是那些正在服药的人，这种想要逃避狂热生活带来的高潮与低谷的愿望，在其他人身上也同样存在。许多人选择了一种更平稳、更枯燥的生活，因为这也意味着更清醒的生活，或者是因为这已是他们力所能及的一切。今天我们的节目主题是《逃避高昂的生活代价》，关注那些从某种重大生活经历中退出来的人们，以及为什么有时这可能是唯一可能的选择。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和国际公共广播电台，这里是《这个美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:05:42.040 --> 00:06:22.030
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our program today. How to Avoid the Everyday Irritations of Everyday Life. Jack Hitt talks to a woman who stumbled onto one way, accidentally and against her will. Act Two, How to Avoid the Difficulty of Living With Someone, in which we hear a philosophical defense of baby talk among adults, that's right, baby talk among adults. We hear the grisly details. And do not pretend that you have not been there, my friend. Act Three, How to Avoid Everything. Writer Dani Shapiro explains how a 22-year-old can avoid facing all the important facts about his or her own life. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的第一幕。《如何逃避日常生活中的日常烦恼》。杰克·希特采访了一位偶然发现了一种方法的女士，虽然这是违背她意愿的。第二幕，《如何逃避与人相处的困难》，我们将听到对成年人之间“宝宝语”的哲学辩护，没错，就是成年人之间的宝宝语。我们将听到其中的细节。别假装你没经历过，我的朋友。第三幕，《如何逃避一切》。作家丹妮·夏皮罗将解释一个22岁的年轻人如何逃避面对他或她生命中所有重要的事实。请继续收听。

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:35.270
<v Ira Glass>Act One, How to Avoid the Everyday Irritations of Everyday Life. No one is in a better position to talk about deadening yourself to life than someone who was forced to do it by nearly dying. Jack Hitt tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《如何逃避日常生活中的日常烦恼》。没有人比那些因濒临死亡而被迫变得麻木的人更有资格谈论如何让自己对生活麻木了。杰克·希特讲述了这个故事。

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:50.840
<v Jack Hitt>Back in the 1980s, when Charlene Riling was just out of college, her life was consumed with being a jock. She played basketball until midnight and could always be counted on for a pickup game of baseball or softball. She especially loved her beautiful yellow and purple Bottechia racing bike.
<v Jack Hitt>回到20世纪80年代，当查琳·瑞林刚大学毕业时，她的生活完全被运动占据了。她打篮球直到午夜，而且在随叫随到的棒球或垒球比赛中总少不了她的身影。她尤其钟爱她那辆漂亮的黄紫色Bottechia赛车。

00:06:50.840 --> 00:07:27.150
<v Charlene Riling>It would be nothing to be riding on the Blue Ridge Parkway down in North Carolina and Tennessee, going down on your racing bike, 50, 55 miles an hour. I don't know that I would do that today. So, yes, I felt very invincible and, in that sense, very confident, really that fearless mentality. Which right now, I have to say, subsequent to everything that happened, I've really lost that.
<v Charlene Riling>在北卡罗来纳州和田纳西州的蓝岭大道上骑行，骑着赛车以时速50到55英里的速度飞驰，这在当时根本不算什么。我不知道今天我还会不会那样做。所以，是的，我当时感觉自己所向无敌，那种感觉非常自信，真的有一种无畏的心态。我不得不说，在经历了这一切之后，我已经彻底失去了那种心态。

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:40.000
<v Jack Hitt>In 1981, Charlene's father died. She'd always thought of him as her best friend, and it almost seemed destined to be that way. He was Charles. She was Charlene. They had the same birthday. She was devastated.
<v Jack Hitt>1981年，查琳的父亲去世了。她一直把他视为自己最好的朋友，而且这似乎是注定的。他叫查尔斯，她叫查琳。他们同一天生日。她彻底崩溃了。

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:54.090
<v Jack Hitt>Then her girlfriend left her. She started to drink a good bit more and lose herself in the bar scene. A couple of times, she and some friends shot up cocaine for a nice, potent, '80s-style high. Then she cleaned up, went to AA.
<v Jack Hitt>随后她的女友也离开了她。她开始喝更多的酒，沉溺于酒吧生活。有几次，她和一些朋友为了寻求80年代那种强烈的快感而注射了可卡因。后来她洗心革面，加入了戒酒互助会。

00:07:54.090 --> 00:08:19.600
<v Jack Hitt>Years later, in the summer of 1988, the invincible Charlene came down with a weird flu she just couldn't shake. It went on for weeks, then months. She had her suspicions, which flared into a private panic when she found out that a guy she'd shared needles with was positive for HIV. She screwed up the courage to go to the county health department to get an anonymous test, and returned on a cold, gloomy day to receive the results.
<v Jack Hitt>多年后，在1988年的夏天，这位“不可战胜”的查琳染上了一种怪异的流感，怎么也治不好。这持续了几个星期，然后是几个月。她心里有了怀疑，当发现曾经和她共用过针头的那个男人艾滋病毒检测呈阳性时，她陷入了私下的恐慌。她鼓起勇气去县卫生局做了匿名检测，并在一个寒冷阴郁的日子回来获取结果。

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:33.640
<v Charlene Riling>I remember sitting out in this big, open waiting room. And there wasn't really anybody in there. And I don't remember how many minutes, but it wasn't that long after sitting down that I was told that I was positive at that time.
<v Charlene Riling>我记得当时坐在那个宽敞的候诊室里。里面几乎没有人。我不记得具体过了多少分钟，但坐下没多久，我就被告知当时我的结果是阳性。

00:08:33.640 --> 00:09:02.820
<v Charlene Riling>The counselor then proceeded to go on to her own little religious agenda, going on to say that Jesus Christ heals all those who repent their sins, referring to my lesbianism as being a sin, and that people are cured of AIDS. And AIDS is a sinful disease, and that's how people get it. But you can be cured of it if you repent your sins.
<v Charlene Riling>随后，那位咨询师开始宣扬她自己的一套宗教议程，继续说道耶稣基督会治愈所有忏悔罪孽的人，她把我的女同性恋身份称为一种罪孽，并说人们是可以治愈艾滋病的。她说艾滋病是一种罪恶的疾病，这就是人们得病的原因。但只要你忏悔自己的罪孽，就能被治愈。

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:30.500
<v Charlene Riling>And it felt like the longest period of my life, being in there, just feeling like you're in one of those little snowy glasses filled with water, and somebody just shook it up. And your head's spinning. And everything's going on. And you want to get out of there, but you can't. And I just felt very trapped.
<v Charlene Riling>那是我一生中最漫长的一段时间，待在那里，感觉自己就像被困在那些盛满水的小雪球装饰品里，被人猛烈地摇晃。大脑一片混乱。所有的事情都在发生。你想离开那里，却走不掉。我感到非常绝望。

00:09:30.500 --> 00:10:10.150
<v Charlene Riling>And then she started talking about this church called Hope Ministries. And Hope Ministries is a place that will convert people from being gay to being straight. And she tried pretty hard to get me to Hope Ministries. And I just took the piece of paper and just left. Never saw the counselor again, never went to Hope Ministries. And that was it.
<v Charlene Riling>然后她开始谈论一个叫“希望事工”的教堂。那个事工是个将同性恋转化为异性恋的地方。她非常卖力地想让我去那个“希望事工”。我只是拿过那张纸就离开了。我再也没见过那个咨询师，也从未去过“希望事工”。事情就是这样。

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:31.470
<v Charlene Riling>I remember the doctor starting the AZT, started on 500 milligrams. And all this bloodwork was being done. And so my whole life, socially, changed. My social, economical class changed.
<v Charlene Riling>我记得医生开始给我开AZT（叠氮胸苷），剂量是500毫克。所有的血液检查都在进行中。我的整个生活，在社交方面，彻底改变了。我的社会和经济阶层也随之改变了。

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.070
<v Jack Hitt>And did you stop working during this time?
<v Jack Hitt>在这期间你停止工作了吗？

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.190
<v Charlene Riling>Yeah, I actually stopped working just shortly afterwards.
<v Charlene Riling>是的，实际上没过多久我就停止工作了。

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:41.040
<v Jack Hitt>So you're 30 years old, and you're forced to retire?
<v Jack Hitt>所以你才30岁，就被迫退休了？

00:10:41.040 --> 00:11:16.910
<v Charlene Riling>Right. And work was the most-- to me, my job was the biggest part of-- it was really my identity, the computer person or computer geek. I loved computers before I ever saw what a computer looked like. I just loved it. It was exciting. And it was about to come out from underneath me very quickly, what my whole life was built on.
<v Charlene Riling>是的。工作对我来说是最重要的——工作实际上就是我的身份，那个搞电脑的人或者电脑极客。在看到电脑长什么样之前，我就爱上了电脑。我就是爱它。那很令人兴奋。而我整个生活建立的基础，很快就要从我脚下坍塌了。

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:30.330
<v Charlene Riling>So you end up with migraines. AZT is known to cause migraines, debilitating migraines. Just bury your head under a pillow, no light. You can't move for days. And they were just awful.
<v Charlene Riling>所以你最终患上了偏头痛。众所周知，AZT会导致偏头痛，那种令人虚脱的偏头痛。只能把头埋在枕头下，不见一丝光亮。你可能连续几天都无法动弹。那种感觉真是太可怕了。

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:39.420
<v Charlene Riling>My muscles had reached a point where they had atrophied. My hands had closed into a fist. My hand was closed, like clenched, like a fist.
<v Charlene Riling>我的肌肉已经萎缩到极限。我的手缩成了拳头。我的手是闭合的，紧紧攥着，像个拳头一样。

00:11:39.420 --> 00:12:00.420
<v Charlene Riling>I could use my finger and my thumb to barely write with. And it looked probably like cat scratch. It wasn't even legible. And I was also having real chronic and debilitating diarrhea, couldn't even make it to the bathroom.
<v Charlene Riling>我只能勉强用手指和拇指写字。那看起来可能就像猫抓的一样。甚至都无法辨认。我还出现了严重的慢性腹泻，令人虚脱，甚至连厕所都赶不上。

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:34.430
<v Charlene Riling>And at some point, Prednisone was introduced, which causes muscle wasting and a host of other side effects. And then the combination of all the other medications, in July of 1991, I was admitted to the hospital for changes in mental status. I was diagnosed as having AIDS dementia, AIDS-related dementia.
<v Charlene Riling>在某个阶段，开始使用了强的松，这会导致肌肉萎缩和一系列其他副作用。在所有其他药物的共同作用下，1991年7月，我因精神状态改变被送进医院。我被诊断患有艾滋病痴呆症，即艾滋病相关痴呆。

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:47.790
<v Charlene Riling>Everything, on one hand, seemed hurried. Death seemed much more inevitable than it just does in daily living. We don't really think about it. We don't plan for our death.
<v Charlene Riling>一方面，一切似乎都很匆忙。死亡看起来比日常生活中要不可避免得多。我们平时并不真正去想它。我们不为自己的死亡做计划。

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:58.520
<v Charlene Riling>But I had gotten a dog during that time. He's a beagle. His name is Sam. He's nine years old now. And it's like Sam knew. Because Sam was around me the most, because he was home, and I was home.
<v Charlene Riling>但我那时候养了一只狗。它是只比格犬。它的名字叫山姆。它现在九岁了。感觉山姆知道。因为山姆大部分时间都陪着我，因为它在家，我也在家。

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:24.770
<v Charlene Riling>And it's like I can remember being in bed and crying, just being so afraid of dying. And I couldn't talk about it with my mother. And I really wanted to. But I knew how my mother felt about death. And I didn't want her to know that I was afraid.
<v Charlene Riling>我记得躺在床上哭泣，因为太害怕死亡。我无法和母亲谈论这件事。我真的很想谈。但我知道母亲对死亡的看法。我不想让她知道我害怕。

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:54.770
<v Charlene Riling>And my biggest fear was what was going to happen with Sam. I used to think about-- Sam used to get so excited when I'd walk in the house. You'd think I was gone for months. I would be gone for a half a day. And it would be like he'd go nuts. And when I came home from the hospital one time, the July '91 hospitalization, and a couple of friends dropped me off. And Sam went nuts all over me.
<v Charlene Riling>我最大的恐惧是山姆以后怎么办。我以前常想——山姆以前每次我进屋时都会非常兴奋。你会以为我离开好几个月了。其实我只是离开半天而已。它会像疯了一样。有一次我从医院回来，那是1991年7月那次住院，几个朋友把我送回来。山姆激动地扑到我身上，高兴坏了。

00:13:54.770 --> 00:14:24.010
<v Charlene Riling>And I kept thinking to myself, when I'm gone, I'm not going to walk back in that door again. And Sam's going to just be waiting for me to come in. And I'm not going to come back in. And I know people might say, "Oh, it's just a dog," but--
<v Charlene Riling>我当时一直在想，当我离开后，我就再也不会走进那扇门了。而山姆还会一直等着我进来。但我再也不会回来了。我知道人们可能会说，“哦，那只是一条狗而已”，但是——

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:43.360
<v Jack Hitt>Charlene prepared to die. The doctors doubled her AZT and told her she had six months to a year to live. She wrote a will at age 31, appointed an executor to her estate, made arrangements with a hospice to care for her. She said goodbye to all her friends, gave away all her most valued possessions.
<v Jack Hitt>查琳为死亡做好了准备。医生将她的AZT剂量加倍，并告诉她只有六个月到一年的生命了。她在31岁时立下了遗嘱，为她的遗产指定了执行人，并安排好了临终关怀中心照顾她。她与所有的朋友告别，送走了所有她最珍视的财物。

00:14:43.360 --> 00:15:06.660
<v Charlene Riling>I remember when you walked into my little beach cottage, on the right hand wall, I had all my bicycle racing numbers. And this whole wall was just all bicycle memorabilia kind of thing, and pictures of me from when I used to ride and race. And I had kept that up.
<v Charlene Riling>我记得当你走进我的小海边小屋时，右手边的墙上，贴满了我的自行车比赛编号。整面墙都是自行车纪念品，还有我以前骑行和比赛时的照片。我一直把它们挂在那。

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:50.980
<v Charlene Riling>But I remember one day when I knew that I was never going to be riding again and never be racing again, and just let that anger set in. And I remember just taking all that stuff down, and probably not very gently either, and just feeling like, "That's all gone. It's all gone, and it's never going to happen again." And I just couldn't look at it anymore. It was too painful.
<v Charlene Riling>但我记得有一天，当我知道我再也不会骑车，再也不会比赛时，我就任由愤怒吞噬了我。我记得我把那些东西全都取了下来，动作大概也不怎么轻柔，只觉得：“一切都结束了。都结束了，再也不会发生了。”我实在无法再看它们一眼。那太痛苦了。

00:15:50.980 --> 00:16:05.750
<v Jack Hitt>In her final days, in a lot of pain, Charlene sought out a renegade physician named Gary Blick in Greenwich, Connecticut. He was known for helping AIDS patients get access to often unorthodox therapies. Her regular doctor suggested she avoid him, called him a quack.
<v Jack Hitt>在最后的日子里，痛苦不堪的查琳在康涅狄格州格林威治寻找了一位名叫加里·布利克的非主流医生。他以帮助艾滋病患者获得非常规疗法而闻名。她的常规医生建议她避开他，称他是个骗子。

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:16.030
<v Jack Hitt>But she was desperate. So she got her friend, Jamie, to drive her down there. And Dr. Blick, as with each new patient, gave her a full workup, including basic blood tests.
<v Jack Hitt>但她已经绝望了。于是她让朋友杰米开车带她过去。布利克医生像对待每位新病人一样，为她做了全面的检查，包括基础血液检测。

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:34.830
<v Charlene Riling>And I remember sitting there, next to Jamie. And we're both sitting in Gary's office. And you sit out, and you look out. And you see the trees out in the back. And I remember it was a sunny day. It was in spring and everything. There was life outside.
<v Charlene Riling>我记得当时坐在杰米旁边。我们俩都坐在加里的办公室里。你坐在那里往外看。你可以看到外面的树木。我记得那是个晴天。当时是春天，一切都很美好。外面充满生机。

00:16:34.830 --> 00:17:02.950
<v Charlene Riling>And sitting there with Jamie, and Dr. Blick sitting there and telling me, "I have good news for you." And he sat there and told me this good news, that I didn't have HIV, and I didn't have AIDS. And I just sat there like, "This can't be."
<v Charlene Riling>我坐在杰米旁边，布利克医生坐在那里告诉我：“我有个好消息要告诉你。”他坐在那里告诉了我这个好消息，说我没有艾滋病毒，也没有艾滋病。我就那么坐着，心想：“这不可能。”

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:08.310
<v Charlene Riling>I've been told this. My whole life's been planned about this. You know what I'm saying.
<v Charlene Riling>我一直被这样告知。我整个人生都是围绕这一点规划的。你懂我的意思吧。

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:12.690
<v Jack Hitt>Did you just assume that he was wrong? Or did you immediately believe him?
<v Jack Hitt>你当时是认为他错了？还是你立刻相信了他？

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:29.380
<v Charlene Riling>No, I don't know. I don't think that I immediately-- I just couldn't. How do you take one day and compare it to three years?
<v Charlene Riling>不，我不知道。我觉得我无法立刻——我只是做不到。你如何用一天的时间去推翻这三年？

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:47.990
<v Jack Hitt>One of the classic stages of dying is denial, refusing to accept that you actually have AIDS, fervently believing that the doctor will step out of the lab and say, "Oh, sorry. It's all been a big mistake. You don't have AIDS." And that is precisely what happened to Charlene, after living as an HIV-positive woman for three years.
<v Jack Hitt>死亡的经典阶段之一是否认，拒绝接受自己真的患有艾滋病，狂热地相信医生会从实验室走出来说：“哦，抱歉。这一切都是个大错误。你没有艾滋病。”而这正是查琳在以艾滋病阳性身份生活了三年后所发生的事情。

00:17:47.990 --> 00:18:10.660
<v Jack Hitt>It turned out that back when she first got tested for HIV, the woman at the county health department had deliberately lied to her, told Charlene she was HIV-positive when in fact, the test was negative. She was hoping that Charlene would come to the little ministry church in town and be healed of both her lesbianism and her AIDS. Then, of course, she'd be a documented, verifiable miracle for Jesus.
<v Jack Hitt>事实证明，当她最初接受艾滋病检测时，县卫生局的那名女子蓄意对她撒了谎，告诉查琳她是艾滋病阳性，而实际上，检测结果是阴性的。她希望查琳会来到镇上的那个小型福音教堂，从而治愈她的同性恋和艾滋病。然后，当然，她就会成为耶稣所创造的一个有据可查、可被证实的奇迹。

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:53.640
<v Charlene Riling>I just broke down and just cried and cried and cried, and just couldn't believe it that I had been betrayed. And I really had been lied to, and that this whole illusion was created for religion, to prove a point, to get me to go to Hope Ministries, to convert from being a lesbian to being straight, and then probably to be set up to be retested again and be told I was negative.
<v Charlene Riling>我彻底崩溃了，哭啊哭啊，无法相信自己竟然被背叛了。我确实被骗了，而这一切幻象都是为了宗教目的而制造出来的，为了证明某个观点，为了诱导我去“希望事工”，为了让我从同性恋转变成异性恋，然后大概会安排我再测一次，再告诉我是阴性。

00:18:53.640 --> 00:19:24.510
<v Charlene Riling>It was more than just losing three years of my life. There was a whole identity and a whole life built around a person with AIDS. I have lived as a person with AIDS. It's the medications that were prescribed, the diagnosis, all the events that took place, AIDS Buddy, AIDS Interfaith, living positively. My whole life was lived as a person with AIDS.
<v Charlene Riling>这不仅仅是失去了我三年的生命。我整个人生和身份都是围绕着一个艾滋病患者建立起来的。我一直作为艾滋病患者生活。无论是处方药、诊断结果，还是发生的所有事件，包括艾滋病互助小组、艾滋病宗教间组织，以及积极生活。我整个人生都是作为艾滋病患者度过的。

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:45.720
<v Jack Hitt>The AIDS drugs were so powerful, they'd given her the symptoms of a person with AIDS. In addition to AZT, Charlene was fed very powerful steroids, which can bring on dementia-like states. But now she had to be weaned off her complicated drug regimen. Cold turkey would definitely kill her. So she spent a year tapering off her prescription drugs until she could once again enter normal life.
<v Jack Hitt>这些艾滋病药物太强了，以至于让她出现了艾滋病人的症状。除了AZT，查琳还服用了非常强力的类固醇，这会引发类似痴呆的状态。但现在她必须戒掉复杂的药物方案。直接停药肯定会要了她的命。于是她花了一年时间逐渐减少处方药的剂量，直到她能重新过上正常生活。

00:19:45.720 --> 00:20:26.220
<v Jack Hitt>But how does one do that? When Charlene found out she was destined to die, she discovered huge institutions ready to help her, the church, the hospices, the AIDS buddy groups. But now she was told to make a U-turn, to go back to the living, to abandon all her moribund friends, to forget the big philosophical questions and return to a life where she could buy a car and sweat out a mortgage, where she could take a lover safely and get annoyed with her, argue with her about the bills and feeding the dog and cleaning up after dinner and mowing in the back yard. Looking back on it all from a nice, cozy room in a new house in Connecticut, she said in many ways, it was easier dying.
<v Jack Hitt>但一个人该怎么做呢？当查琳发现自己注定要死时，她发现了庞大的机构随时准备帮助她，教会、临终关怀中心、艾滋病互助小组。但现在她被告知要掉头，回到生者的世界，抛弃所有垂死的朋友，忘记宏大的哲学问题，回到那个她可以买车、为房贷奔波的生活中，回到那个她可以安全地找个爱人，跟她吵架，为账单、喂狗、饭后清理和修剪后院草坪而争论的生活。从她在康涅狄格州新家的一间舒适房间里回望这一切，她说在很多方面，其实死亡反而更容易。

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:45.730
<v Charlene Riling>You know, you start letting go of life's little things that might have set you off or whatever. Those things in life seemed just so insignificant. There was something much bigger than that that was happening.
<v Charlene Riling>你知道，你开始放下生活中那些可能让你抓狂的小事。生活中的那些事情看起来是如此微不足道。当时正在发生的事情远比那些重要得多。

00:20:45.730 --> 00:21:27.390
<v Charlene Riling>The work that it took to get to the edge, and to have gotten really to the end, essentially, to get that far and then to be pulled back. There was one part of me, that wait-- there's some simplicity here. There's some peacefulness here. There's no more insanity of the world and people's politics and people's pushiness, and their beliefs.
<v Charlene Riling>为了走到边缘，真正走到尽头，经历了这一切，然后又被拉回来。我心中有一部分觉得——这里有一种简单。这里有一种平静。世界上再也没有了那些疯狂、人们的政治、人们的急躁和他们的各种信仰。

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:49.940
<v Charlene Riling>I work hard to keep my life simple today. But I wanted to keep it as simple as it was in that preparation for dying. And you can't do that and live in the real world at the same time.
<v Charlene Riling>我今天努力让生活保持简单。但我曾想让它像为死亡做准备时那样简单。而要在现实世界中生活，你是不可能做到这一点的。

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:53.790
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt, who put that story together, is a contributing editor to This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特，策划这个故事的人，是《这个美国生活》的特约编辑。

00:21:53.790 --> 00:22:13.550
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "CALIFORNIA" BY QUASI]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - Quasi 的 "California"]

00:22:13.550 --> 00:23:24.660
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, the social uses of baby talk. That's baby talk among adults. Brace yourself, because it's just one minute away, from Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，宝宝语的社会用途。我说的是成年人之间的宝宝语。做好心理准备，因为再过一分钟就到，来自国际公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:53.270
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of writers and reporters to tackle that theme. Today's program, Avoiding the  High Cost of Living. These are stories-- I realize, actually, I've avoided saying this because when you say it out loud, it sounds so depressing. These are stories about people who choose not to live every moment to the fullest, who choose to withdraw from life in some way, to make themselves numb, and stories about why that's sometimes the only choice possible.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，邀请各类作家和记者来探讨这个主题。今天节目的主题是《逃避高昂的生活代价》。这些故事——其实我意识到，我一直回避说出这点，因为当你大声说出来时，听起来太压抑了。这些故事是关于那些选择不尽情享受生活每一刻的人，那些选择以某种方式退缩、让自己麻木的人，以及关于为什么这有时是唯一可能的选择的故事。

00:23:53.270 --> 00:24:14.750
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program, Act Two, How to Avoid the Difficulty of Living With Someone. "Cuteness," a friend with small children once said to me, "is what nature gives to our offspring so we don't kill them." Without cuteness, our species dies off. But then what is the function of cuteness among adults?
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第二幕，第二幕，《如何逃避与人相处的困难》。“可爱”，一位有小孩的朋友曾对我说，“是自然赋予我们后代的东西，这样我们才不会杀了他们。”没有可爱，我们的物种就会灭绝。但成年人之间可爱的功能又是什么呢？

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:30.860
<v Ira Glass>Consider, please, the role of baby talk among adults. This is speech designed specifically and entirely for cuteness, shared in private. And as I've said, our program today is about all the things that we do to actually avoid experiencing our own lives.
<v Ira Glass>请考虑一下成年人之间“宝宝语”的作用。这种说话方式专为可爱而设计，完全在私下分享。正如我所说，我们今天的节目是关于我们为了逃避体验自己的生活而所做的一切事情。

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:46.460
<v Ira Glass>I would argue that baby talk, cute speech, is one way that we do that. People seldom discuss this in public. Our own producer, Nancy Updike, and her boyfriend, Chicago journalist Adam Davidson, agreed to step forward and speak the truth about the issue.
<v Ira Glass>我想说，宝宝语，这种可爱的说话方式，是我们逃避生活的一种手段。人们很少在公开场合讨论这一点。我们的制片人南希·厄普代克和她的男友，芝加哥记者亚当·戴维森，同意站出来讲述关于这个问题的真相。

00:24:46.460 --> 00:25:21.140
<v Nancy Updike>You want a shortcut to the intimacy that used to come from just spending hours and hours talking to one another about our lives. Because when you're first falling in love, that's the only thing that you do, and the only thing that you want to do. And you make as much time as possible for just talking to each other. And then as the relationship progresses and other things reassert themselves, work or friends or whatever it is, then you have less time together.
<v Nancy Updike>你想走捷径来获得那种曾经只需花数小时聊天就能获得的亲密感。因为当你刚坠入爱河时，那是你唯一会做的事，也是你唯一想做的事。你会尽可能多地腾出时间来只和对方说话。而随着关系的深入，其他事情——工作、朋友之类——重新占据了你的生活，那么你们在一起的时间就少了。

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:40.530
<v Nancy Updike>And you want to get that intimacy. And you don't have as much time. So you're trying to sort of make a shortcut to it.
<v Nancy Updike>你想要获得那种亲密度，但又没有那么多时间。所以你试图走个捷径。

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:58.670
<v Ira Glass>It happened so fast. In Adam and Nancy's case, they'd be going out only three months when, like twins, or people in captivity, or deserted tribes on remote desert islands, they began developing their own private language. At some point, they found that they were whispering to each other a lot.
<v Ira Glass>这一切发生得太快了。在亚当和南希的例子中，他们交往才三个月，就像双胞胎，或者被囚禁的人，又或是荒岛上的幸存者一样，他们开始发展出自己的私人语言。在某个时刻，他们发现自己经常对着对方耳语。

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:21.880
<v Nancy Updike>Well, I feel like that came about a lot on the phone, because Adam works at home. So he calls me a lot during the day, and I call him, too. And then we say, "Hi, how are you doing." And I'll say, "I got to go." And Adam will say, "No, don't go." And I'll say, "I have to go." "No, don't go." "But I have to go." "No."
<v Nancy Updike>我觉得那更多是在电话里产生的，因为亚当在家办公。所以他白天经常打电话给我，我也打给他。然后我们说，“嗨，你怎么样。”我会说，“我得走了。”亚当会说，“不，别走。”我会说，“我必须得走。”“不，别走。”“但我必须走。”“不。”

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:23.710
<v Ira Glass>And the key is the whispering.
<v Ira Glass>关键在于耳语。

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:24.350
<v Adam Davidson>The whispering, yeah.
<v Adam Davidson>耳语，是的。

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:29.670
<v Nancy Updike>There's whispering. But it's also like baby talk, like, "No, don't want you to go." "Don't go." "Must go." "Don't go."
<v Nancy Updike>会有耳语。但这更像宝宝语，就像，“不，不想让你走。”“别走。”“必须得走。”“别走。”

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:35.770
<v Ira Glass>And so you're doing that. So that starts on the phone, but then you find that it starts to creep to other areas of your life as well?
<v Ira Glass>所以你们在这样做。那是在电话里开始的，但后来你们发现它开始渗透到你们生活的其他方面了？

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:49.720
<v Adam Davidson>Right. If you wanted to record baby talk, you'd want to put a mic over our bed when we're going to sleep, because that's when we do it the most. And then it's not baby talk, but it's hitting. There's a lot of--
<v Adam Davidson>没错。如果你想录下宝宝语，你会想在我们睡觉时把麦克风放在床上，因为那时我们做得最多。那不只是宝宝语，还有拍打。有很多——

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:52.590
<v Nancy Updike>Oh, this is a classic.
<v Nancy Updike>噢，这很经典。

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:55.590
<v Ira Glass>I should just say that you just sort of hit her. I don't know how to describe--
<v Ira Glass>我应该说你只是轻拍她。我不知道怎么形容——

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:13.270
<v Nancy Updike>But in a very sort of kid way to hit. Not like you'd punch someone. I'd come home at the end of the day and maybe turn on the TV. We're eating dinner and watching TV. And we're kind of quiet.
<v Nancy Updike>但那是一种非常孩子气的拍打。不是像你会挥拳打人那样。我一天工作结束回到家，可能会打开电视。我们边吃晚饭边看电视。我们当时比较安静。

00:27:13.270 --> 00:27:27.840
<v Nancy Updike>And then all the sudden, Adam would just reach out and hit me a little bit. He'd be like "I like you." It was like you would do in first grade. And of course, now I hit back. And I yell, "I like you."
<v Nancy Updike>然后突然间，亚当会伸出手轻轻拍我一下。他会说“我喜欢你”。就像你在一年级时做的那样。当然，现在我也回拍他。我大喊，“我喜欢你。”

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:48.900
<v Adam Davidson>And that's another whole voice, which is different, like, "I like you." Like I'd be operating the thermostat. And Nancy would be like, "Could you turn it down?" And I'd say, "No, it's too cold." And she'd be like, "No, it's the other cold. It's too hot." And that's a whole genre.
<v Adam Davidson>这就是另一种声音了，这不一样，比如，“我喜欢你。”就像我在操作恒温器。南希会说，“你能调低点吗？”我会说，“不行，太冷了。”她会说，“不，是另一种冷。太热了。”这是一整套类型。

00:27:48.900 --> 00:28:07.290
<v Nancy Updike>Did you mean "turn it up"? "No, I meant the other up, down!" This is definitely the most embarrassing thing that I've ever talked about in public, without any question in my mind.
<v Nancy Updike>你是想说“调高”吗？“不，我是说另一个上，向下！”这绝对是我在公共场合谈论过的最尴尬的事情，毫无疑问。

00:28:07.290 --> 00:28:14.930
<v Ira Glass>What's amazing is that you talk about sex in public. You talk about money in public. But this, why this?
<v Ira Glass>令人惊奇的是，你们会在公共场合谈论性。你们会在公共场合谈论钱。但这个，为什么是这个？

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:23.860
<v Nancy Updike>Because it's completely uncool. There's nothing cool about it. There's no way to make it cool.
<v Nancy Updike>因为它完全不酷。它一点也不酷。没办法让它变酷。

00:28:23.860 --> 00:29:02.900
<v Nancy Updike>You can be outrageous about sex. Or you can make fun of either having a lot of money or having no money. There are ways that you can talk about those things that you can preserve some corner of dignity. But baby talk is completely without any cool or dignity or any sort of redeeming social value. It is truly a dirty little secret, much more than sex.
<v Nancy Updike>你可以对性表现得离经叛道。或者你可以拿有很多钱或没钱来开玩笑。有很多谈论这些事情的方式可以让你保留一点尊严。但宝宝语完全不酷，没有尊严，也没有任何社会价值。它确实是一个肮脏的小秘密，比性还要秘密。

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:09.360
<v Ira Glass>And it happens in this protected space, namely privacy.
<v Ira Glass>而且它发生在这样一个受保护的空间里，即隐私空间。

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:37.490
<v Nancy Updike>Right, right, exactly. And it's a bit strange, because it's this very performative speech. It is a performance. It's like kabuki. You do it the same way every time. And everyone knows the next move. But it's this performance for one person.
<v Nancy Updike>对，对，没错。而且这有点奇怪，因为它是一种表演性极强的语言。这是一场表演。就像歌舞伎。你每次都用同样的方式表演。每个人都知道下一步动作。但这是为了一个人表演。

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:42.600
<v Adam Davidson>And then there was this thing where one of us was nicknamed "Schnookie."
<v Adam Davidson>然后还有一件事情，你们其中一人被昵称为“Schnookie”。

