00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:49.380
<v Adam Davidson>Nancy would always know which one was, and I would always forget who--
<v Adam Davidson>南希总是知道哪一个是，但我总是忘记谁是——

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:51.620
<v Nancy Updike>Well, there was-- OK, if we're going to do this--
<v Nancy Updike>好吧，如果有——好吧，如果我们要说这个——

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:56.000
<v Ira Glass>One of you had a nickname. And there were only two of you in the relationship, and you couldn't remember which was which?
<v Ira Glass>你们其中一个人有个昵称。你们关系里只有两个人，你们竟然记不清谁是谁？

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:14.710
<v Nancy Updike>No, no, no. One was Schnookie. And then the other one, of course, it's Schlumpy. Because one of you is Schnookie, so logically, the other one must be Schlumpy. Tears of embarrassment are springing to my eyes.
<v Nancy Updike>不，不，不。一个是Schnookie。然后另一个，当然，就是Schlumpy。因为其中一个是Schnookie，所以从逻辑上讲，另一个必须是Schlumpy。尴尬的泪水涌上我的眼眶。

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:27.830
<v Adam Davidson>But then that becomes a whole thing. Like, "No, wait, are you Schnookie? Hey, Schnook. G'night, Schnook."
<v Adam Davidson>但这随后变成了一整套东西。比如，“不，等等，你是Schnookie吗？嘿，Schnook。晚安，Schnook。”

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:48.480
<v Nancy Updike>I remember that we were lying in bed together. And we were going back and forth. We were just talking before going to sleep. And we were going back and forth in one of the ways that we had at that time.
<v Nancy Updike>我记得我们躺在床上。我们来回说着话。睡前我们只是聊天。我们用当时那种方式来回交谈。

00:30:48.480 --> 00:31:03.400
<v Nancy Updike>"You're taking the covers." "No, you." "I want that blanket. No, that blanket, the other blanket, the one blanket." "Come here, Schlumpy." "Oh, Schnookie." Whatever it was.
<v Nancy Updike>“你在抢被子。”“不，是你。”“我要那条毯子。不，那条毯子，另一条毯子，那条毯子。”“过来，Schlumpy。”“噢，Schnookie。”不管是什么。

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:25.190
<v Nancy Updike>And I just felt like I was going to cry. I just felt so full of despair. And I said, "Can we stop talking this way?" And then, what did you say?
<v Nancy Updike>我当时觉得我快要哭了。我感到内心充满了绝望。我说，“我们能停止这样说话吗？”然后，你说了什么？

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:46.050
<v Adam Davidson>Well, I remember feeling like been a tough few weeks. Each of us had had a lot of tough times in work, and didn't have a lot of time together, because we were so busy. And I remember it had been days of just me feeling like, "I don't even know her, and she doesn't know--" And when you're that close, you just feel like, "I don't know me."
<v Adam Davidson>嗯，我记得那几周过得很艰难。我们俩在工作中都遇到了很多困难，在一起的时间很少，因为我们太忙了。我记得那是连续几天，我感觉“我甚至不认识她，而她也不认识——”当你亲近到那种程度，你会觉得，“我甚至不认识我自己。”

00:31:46.050 --> 00:32:03.540
<v Adam Davidson>And I just had wanted to say something about this baby talk for so long. But first of all, I couldn't think to say anything outside of baby talk. And second of all, I thought it would really hurt her. And so when she said it, it was just, what a relief.
<v Adam Davidson>我真的很想说出关于这些宝宝语的事情，但我首先想不出除了宝宝语之外该说什么。其次，我以为这真的会伤害她。所以当她说出来时，那真是一种解脱。

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:39.970
<v Adam Davidson>And I remember beginning to talk like, "You're right. We should talk in a serious way." And it was like finding a new mouth and finding a new tongue and finding a new way of moving all of it and expressing myself that felt, in a way, more false than the baby talk, just because it felt unpracticed and artificial and deliberate.
<v Adam Davidson>我记得开始说话就像，“你是对的。我们应该认真地交流。”那感觉就像找到了一个新的嘴，一个新的舌头，一个新的表达方式，这让我觉得，在某种程度上，比宝宝语更虚假，仅仅因为它感觉生疏、人工且刻意。

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:48.820
<v Ira Glass>You know, when you're in a couple, you're both actually in a couple. And then you're pretending to be in a couple at the same time.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，当你处在一段关系中，你们实际上是在一段关系中。同时你们又在假装处在一段关系中。

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:52.790
<v Nancy Updike>Yeah, totally. Yeah, we've talked about this.
<v Nancy Updike>是的，完全是。是的，我们讨论过这个。

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.890
<v Ira Glass>You have? What do you say?
<v Ira Glass>你们讨论过？你们说什么？

00:32:54.890 --> 00:33:20.860
<v Adam Davidson>I think it's really nice to pretend sometimes. Like sometimes, being in a mature relationship is just not what I want to do, because pretending does have its advantages sometimes. Pretending does let you avoid things for a couple days, which sometimes is what you need and is OK.
<v Adam Davidson>我觉得有时候假装一下真的很好。有时候，处在一段成熟的关系中并不是我想要的，因为假装有时确实有它的好处。假装能让你逃避现实几天，而这有时正是你所需要的，也没什么大不了。

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:40.790
<v Nancy Updike>I think fake intimacy that seems real is much easier, and it's much more seamless. Because there's nothing to disrupt the flow of it. You never get angry, and you never hate each other.
<v Nancy Updike>我觉得看似真实的虚假亲密关系更容易，也更天衣无缝。因为没有什么能破坏它的流动性。你永远不会生气，永远不会互相厌恶。

00:33:40.790 --> 00:34:04.820
<v Nancy Updike>And you're never just annoyed. And you're never bored. And you're never anything that would interrupt that readout of intimacy. Nothing like that ever happens. And then, yeah, it's easier to pretend to be a happy couple than to be a happy couple.
<v Nancy Updike>你也永远不会只是恼火。你永远不会厌倦。你永远不会有任何会中断那种亲密感的事情。那样的事情永远不会发生。然后，是的，假装是一对幸福的伴侣比真正做一对幸福的伴侣更容易。

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:06.540
<v Ira Glass>It's easier to baby talk.
<v Ira Glass>说宝宝语更容易。

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:07.040
<v Nancy Updike>Yeah.
<v Nancy Updike>是的。

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:13.990
<v Adam Davidson>Yeah.
<v Adam Davidson>是的。

00:34:13.990 --> 00:34:18.260
<v Ira Glass>Did you feel and do you feel that you're actually in danger, that there's something that you could lose?
<v Ira Glass>你是否觉得，而且你现在是否觉得你们实际上处于危险之中，有些东西你们可能会失去？

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:20.090
<v Nancy Updike>Yes, definitely.
<v Nancy Updike>是的，肯定的。

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:22.200
<v Ira Glass>And what is the thing that you could lose?
<v Ira Glass>你们可能会失去的东西是什么？

00:34:22.200 --> 00:34:36.280
<v Nancy Updike>The actual the substance of the relationship. That sounds too cold, but I felt like I'm losing this person that I fell in love with and that I am in love with.
<v Nancy Updike>关系的实质。这听起来太冷酷了，但我感觉我正在失去这个我坠入爱河并与之相爱的人。

00:34:36.280 --> 00:35:00.490
<v Adam Davidson>When I'm in that kind of baby talk, pretend intimacy, it's impossible to imagine real intimacy and real connection, or just a real conversation. Even in my mind, I think of it through that baby talk. Just remembering, real conversations are suddenly baby talk conversations.
<v Adam Davidson>当我处于那种宝宝语、假装的亲密状态时，想象真实的亲密和真正的联系，或者仅仅是一场真正的谈话都是不可能的。甚至在我的脑海中，我也会通过那种宝宝语来思考。回想起来，真正的谈话突然都变成了宝宝语谈话。

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:09.880
<v Nancy Updike>"I want a good relationship." "No, I want a good relationship."
<v Nancy Updike>“我想要一段好的关系。”“不，我想要一段好的关系。”

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:16.120
<v Adam Davidson>Well, I remember when this happened, the first time it happened, because of course, now it happens twice a week.
<v Adam Davidson>嗯，我记得当这事发生时，第一次发生的时候，因为当然，现在它一周发生两次。

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:20.240
<v Ira Glass>It happens twice a week that you actually have to say, "Oh, wait, now I will be an adult again"?
<v Ira Glass>你们一周有两次实际上必须说，“哦，等等，现在我又要变回成年人了”？

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:22.130
<v Adam Davidson>I think it depends. We're pretty--
<v Adam Davidson>我想这取决于情况。我们相当——

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:22.400
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:22.900
<v Nancy Updike>Yeah.
<v Nancy Updike>是的。

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:28.600
<v Ira Glass>Oh, that's so interesting. You mean that the pull of this sort of fakey--
<v Ira Glass>噢，那太有趣了。你是说这种虚假的——

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:36.200
<v Nancy Updike>No, it 's a chronic condition, definitely. It's like TB. Once you test positive, you have to always watch for it.
<v Nancy Updike>不，这是一种慢性病，肯定的。就像肺结核。一旦检测呈阳性，你必须时刻提防。

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:44.930
<v Adam Davidson>Because it's just like a warm bath. It's the easiest thing in the world to do. It's just like falling down and just hitting the ground.
<v Adam Davidson>因为它就像一个温暖的浴缸。这是世界上最容易做的事情。就像摔倒在地一样简单。

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:59.470
<v Nancy Updike>It's like a vaudeville routine that the two have you been doing together for 30 years. He says his line. You say your line. The audience laughs. Everything happens the way it's supposed to.
<v Nancy Updike>这就像一段你们俩已经一起表演了30年的杂耍节目。他说他的台词。你说你的台词。观众笑了。一切都按照预想的那样进行。

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:00.910
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike and Adam Davidson.
<v Ira Glass>南希·厄普代克和亚当·戴维森。

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:34.890
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "BABY TALK" BY BILLY IDOL]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 比利·爱多的 "Baby Talk"]

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:51.470
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, How to Avoid Everything. It's possible to turn off your emotions and go into autopilot for brief periods with the person you love. And it's also possible to shut your feelings down completely, as in this memoir from writer Dani Shapiro.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《如何逃避一切》。在与爱人相处时，暂时关闭情感并进入自动驾驶状态是可能的。完全关闭你的感觉也是可能的，正如作家丹妮·夏皮罗的这篇回忆录所述。

00:36:51.470 --> 00:37:09.210
<v Dani Shapiro>Here, in no particular order, are some things Lenny told me. That he and his wife didn't sleep in the same bed, that they hadn't had a real marriage in years. That she was undergoing electroshock treatment in a clinic outside Philadelphia. That he had cancer and had to fly to Houston three days a week for chemotherapy.
<v Dani Shapiro>以下是莱尼告诉我的事情，没有特定顺序。他和妻子不在同一张床上睡觉，他们多年来都没有过真正的婚姻生活。她正在费城郊外的一家诊所接受电击治疗。他患有癌症，每周必须飞三次休斯敦接受化疗。

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:22.320
<v Dani Shapiro>That his youngest daughter, age three, had a rare form of childhood leukemia. That he could not get a divorce for all of the above reasons. That he was heartbroken that he could not leave his wife and marry me.
<v Dani Shapiro>他三岁的小女儿患有一种罕见的儿童白血病。由于上述所有原因，他无法离婚。他很心碎，因为他无法离开妻子和我结婚。

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:39.800
<v Dani Shapiro>For a long time, I believed him. With every bone in my body, I trusted that Lenny Klein was telling me the truth. When we talked about it, his jaw would tighten, and his big brown eyes would fill with tears. His voice would quaver with pent-up, complex feelings that I couldn't possibly begin to understand.
<v Dani Shapiro>很长一段时间，我都相信他。我全身心地信任莱尼·克莱恩告诉我的都是真相。当我们谈论这些时，他的下巴会紧绷，他那双棕色的大眼睛里会充满泪水。他的声音会因无法抑制的复杂情绪而颤抖，而那是我根本无法理解的。

00:37:39.800 --> 00:38:06.210
<v Dani Shapiro>Poor Lenny. I marveled that so many bad things could happen to one person. And I vowed to take care of him. Writing late at night in my extensive journals, I exhorted myself to be a real woman, one who could step up to the plate and be good to her man in his moment of crisis.
<v Dani Shapiro>可怜的莱尼。我惊叹于一个人身上竟然会发生这么多不幸的事。我发誓要照顾他。在深夜写下密密麻麻的日记时，我鼓励自己做一个真正的女人，一个能在男人遭遇危机时挺身而出、对他好的女人。

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:56.070
<v Dani Shapiro>Years later, I hold Lenny's lies up to the light and examine my own reasons for believing what, in retrospect, seems preposterous. I reread my old journals and notice the way my girlish handwriting deteriorated into a scrawl as I wrote, "I have to be there for Lenny. He needs me, and he's going through so much. I don't know if I can handle it, but I have to be strong." I try to remember that Lenny was a trial lawyer, that he built an international reputation based on his own pathology, that he lied with an almost evangelical conviction. He prided himself on being able to convince anyone of anything.
<v Dani Shapiro>多年后，我把莱尼的谎言放在光下审视，并检视我自己相信那些事后看来极其荒谬谎言的原因。我重读旧日记，注意到我少女般的笔迹变得潦草，当时我写道：“我必须陪在莱尼身边。他需要我，他经历了这么多。我不知道自己能不能应付，但我必须坚强。”我试着记住莱尼是一名辩护律师，他基于自己的病态建立了国际声誉，他以一种近乎狂热的信念撒谎。他以能说服任何人相信任何事而自豪。

00:38:56.070 --> 00:39:14.850
<v Dani Shapiro>Paris, 1985. We are walking along the Boulevard Saint-Germain on a cloudless spring day. The rooftops of the Left Bank are creamy against a rare blue sky. And the air outside Cafe Flore smells of croissants and the acrid smoke of Gitane. But I don't notice.
<v Dani Shapiro>巴黎，1985年。在一个无云的春日，我们走在圣日耳曼大道上。左岸的屋顶在稀有的蓝天下显得奶油般润泽。花神咖啡馆外空气中弥漫着羊角面包的香气和吉坦香烟刺鼻的烟味。但我没注意到。

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:42.850
<v Dani Shapiro>In Paris, in 1985, I only see what is within one square foot of me, too busy feeling the complicated stew of sensations being with Lenny provokes. I am hung over, floating on a wave of last night's Puligny-Montrachet and a four star dinner that wound up in the toilet of the Hotel Ritz. Lenny's arm is around me, thick and proprietary. I have not read a newspaper or spoken to a soul other than Lenny for weeks now.
<v Dani Shapiro>1985年的巴黎，我只能看到周围一平方英尺内的东西，因为忙于感受与莱尼在一起所引发的复杂心情而无暇他顾。我宿醉未醒，漂浮在昨晚普里尼-蒙哈谢葡萄酒和一顿以酒店厕所收场的四星级晚餐带来的余韵中。莱尼的手搂着我，厚实且占有欲十足。几周来，除了莱尼，我没读过报纸，也没和任何人说过话。

00:39:42.850 --> 00:40:12.550
<v Dani Shapiro>I've been living the kind of unbelievable life people glide through in airport novels. We have been to London, Monte Carlo, the Cote d'Azur. I have played blackjack in private clubs with oil sheiks, who asked me to blow on their dice for good luck. I wear dark glasses and haute couture suits, a gold watch, and a long, thick strand of pearls. I have no idea who I am.
<v Dani Shapiro>我过着人们在机场小说中才会滑过的难以置信的生活。我们去过伦敦、蒙特卡洛、蔚蓝海岸。我和石油大亨在私人俱乐部玩过二十一点，他们让我对着骰子吹气祈求好运。我戴着深色墨镜，穿着高级定制套装，戴着金表和一长串珍珠项链。我不知道我是谁。

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:38.630
<v Dani Shapiro>Lenny steers us onto a narrow side street off the Boulevard Saint-Germain and into a children's clothing store, filled with embroidered dresses my mother used to buy me as a child. He tells me he wants to buy a dress for his youngest daughter, the one with the rare form of leukemia. I help him look through racks of tiny dresses suitable for a three-year-old until we find one he deems perfect, a pale, yellow silk smock with a Peter Pan collar.
<v Dani Shapiro>莱尼带着我们拐进圣日耳曼大道旁的一条小街，走进一家童装店，里面装满了母亲小时候常给我买的那种绣花裙子。他告诉我他想为他最小的女儿买一条裙子，就是那个患有罕见白血病的女儿。我帮他翻看架子上那些适合三岁孩子穿的小裙子，直到我们找到了他认为完美的款式，一件带有彼得潘领的淡黄色丝绸罩衫。

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:56.310
<v Dani Shapiro>He holds it up to the sunlight, and his eyes fill with tears. "She'll never live to outgrow this dress," he whispers, "my baby girl." He has layered his lies one on top of the other until they have become opaque, an elaborate construction resembling reality.
<v Dani Shapiro>他把它举到阳光下，眼里满是泪水。“她永远活不到长出这条裙子的时候了，”他低语道，“我的宝贝女儿。”他把谎言一层层堆叠，直至变得不透明，构建成了一个酷似现实的复杂结构。

00:40:56.310 --> 00:41:14.960
<v Dani Shapiro>He is fond of quoting probably the only line he knows from Franz Kafka. "White is black, and black is white," he often says with a sigh. I never knew exactly what he meant by this. But it seemed to have a lot to do with my life at the time.
<v Dani Shapiro>他喜欢引用弗朗茨·卡夫卡大概也是他唯一知道的一句话。“白色是黑色，黑色是白色，”他常常叹着气说。我从不确切知道他这话是什么意思。但它似乎与我当时的生活有很大关系。

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:45.890
<v Dani Shapiro>The lies had small beginnings. Lenny called me from a business trip and told me he was at Montreal Airport, waiting to catch a flight to Calgary. I checked with the airline and found out that the flight would take approximately five hours. So when Lenny called an hour later to say he had landed in Calgary, I very calmly asked him where he really was. "Calgary," he said. "No, Lenny, really." He stuck to his story. In the time that I knew him, he never, ever changed his story midstream.
<v Dani Shapiro>谎言开始于小事。莱尼从出差途中给我打电话，告诉我他在蒙特利尔机场，等着转机去卡尔加里。我向航空公司查询，发现飞行时间大约需要五个小时。所以当莱尼一小时后打电话说他已经降落在卡尔加里时，我非常冷静地问他到底在哪里。“卡尔加里，”他说。“不，莱尼，说实话。”他坚持自己的说法。在我认识他的这段时间里，他从未，绝对从未中途改变过他的谎言。

00:41:45.890 --> 00:42:01.970
<v Dani Shapiro>I hung up on him and called his family's house in Westchester. When the maid answered the phone, I asked to speak with Mr. Klein. And when he picked up the extension, and I heard his rough, craggy "Hello," I screamed so hard into his ear that he dropped the receiver.
<v Dani Shapiro>我挂了他的电话，给他在威彻斯特的家里打了电话。当佣人接电话时，我要求找克莱恩先生。当他拿起分机，听到他粗哑的“喂”声时，我对着他的耳朵尖叫，以至于他丢掉了听筒。

00:42:01.970 --> 00:42:10.720
<v Dani Shapiro>He raced into the city. He let himself into my apartment and found me curled up in bed. He scooped me up and held me to his chest.
<v Dani Shapiro>他驱车赶往城里。他溜进我的公寓，发现我缩在床上。他把我抱起来，紧紧贴在胸口。

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:25.080
<v Dani Shapiro>His wife wasn't home, he told me. She was having shock treatment. And someone had to take care of his daughter. He hadn't wanted to tell me because he'd wanted to spare me, to protect me from the horror of his life. Surely I understood.
<v Dani Shapiro>他告诉我，他妻子不在家。她正在接受电击治疗。有人必须照顾他的女儿。他不想告诉我，因为他想保护我，让我免受他生活恐怖的折磨。难道我不理解吗？

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:46.750
<v Dani Shapiro>"Shh, sweetheart," he murmured into the top of my head as I wept, my face beet red like a little girl's. "So many people need me," he said, "But I love you best of all."
<v Dani Shapiro>“嘘，亲爱的，”当我哭泣时，他在我的头顶低语，我的脸涨得通红，像个小女孩一样。“这么多人需要我，”他说，“但我最爱你。”

00:42:46.750 --> 00:43:12.260
<v Dani Shapiro>Two years have passed, and something has gone wrong, terribly wrong, with my life. I don't, in fact, think of my life as "my life," but rather as a series of random events that have no logical connection. I am no longer a student. I dropped out of Sarah Lawrence after my junior year, supposedly to pursue acting. And I'm actually doing a pretty good imitation of an actress. But I'm doing an even better imitation of a mistress.
<v Dani Shapiro>两年过去了，我的生活出了一些问题，非常严重的问题。事实上，我不认为我的生活是“我的生活”，而是一系列没有逻辑联系的随机事件。我不再是学生了。大三后我从莎拉·劳伦斯学院退学，据说是因为想追求演艺事业。我确实在进行不错的演员模仿秀。但我模仿情妇模仿得更好。

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:36.430
<v Dani Shapiro>Lenny's been busy buying me things. I don't particularly want these things, but they seem to be what Lenny is offering in lieu of himself. So quite suddenly, overnight really, I find myself driving a black Mercedes convertible. And just in case I might be mistaken for anything other than a kept woman, I wear a mink coat, a Cartier watch, a Bulgari necklace with an ancient coin at its center.
<v Dani Shapiro>莱尼一直忙着给我买东西。我并不特别想要这些东西，但它们似乎是莱尼代替他自己所提供的东西。所以很突然地，一夜之间，我发现自己开上了一辆黑色奔驰敞篷车。为了防止被误认为除了被包养的女人之外的任何身份，我穿着貂皮大衣，戴着卡地亚手表，戴着一条以古币为中心的宝格丽项链。

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:59.930
<v Dani Shapiro>The Mercedes is a step down from the first car Lenny gave me when we had been going out for a month, a leased Ferrari. I don't know how to drive a stick shift, so the Ferrari was a bit of a problem. What I must have looked like, a 20-year-old blonde dressed like Ivana Trump, stalled in traffic, grinding gears, trying to find the point on the clutch to hold that ridiculous car in place.
<v Dani Shapiro>这辆奔驰车比我们交往一个月时莱尼给我的第一辆车——一辆租来的法拉利——差了一档。我不知道怎么开手动挡，所以那辆法拉利有点麻烦。我当时看起来一定像个打扮成伊万娜·特朗普的20岁金发女郎，在交通堵塞中熄火，磨损齿轮，试图找到离合器的点来hold住那辆荒唐的车。

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:37.040
<v Dani Shapiro>Lenny rented an apartment on a pretty little street in Greenwich Village, a furnished triplex with a garden, a fireplace, and a bedroom with a four-poster bed. He called it "our house," as if he didn't have another home with a whole family in it an hour north of the city. He kept half a dozen suits in the bedroom closet, and a brand new silk robe hung behind the bathroom door. There was an entire floor we didn't use, a large, airy children's nursery.
<v Dani Shapiro>莱尼在格林威治村一条漂亮的小街上租了一间公寓，一套带花园、壁炉和带四柱床卧室的精装修三层复式公寓。他称之为“我们的家”，仿佛他没有在城北一小时车程外还有另一个带着一大家子的家。他在卧室衣橱里挂了半打西装，浴室门后挂着一件崭新的丝绸睡袍。那里还有一整层我们没用到的空间，一个宽敞、通风的儿童育儿室。

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:46.990
<v Dani Shapiro>My parents knew that something was up. They knew I was going out with somebody, but they had no idea who. I was drifting away from them. And they were letting me go.
<v Dani Shapiro>我父母知道出事了。他们知道我在和某人交往，但不知道是谁。我正在疏远他们。而他们也正在放手。

00:44:46.990 --> 00:45:31.480
<v Dani Shapiro>One night, I invited them over for dinner. I pushed all traces of Lenny out of sight. But of course, there were clues, a glossy brochure for Italian yachts, a humidor in the center of the coffee table, a man's Burberry overcoat on a hook near the front door. I cooked up a storm, and the place was filled with homey smells-- garlic, basil, coriander. It was winter, and the snow was piled up on the sills. Spotlights in the back yard shone on the landscaped garden, the redwood table, the Adirondack chairs, and the huge, terra cotta pots of last spring's dead geraniums. I had my father's favorite music, Dvorak's Symphony for the New World, playing on the stereo system.
<v Dani Shapiro>一天晚上，我邀请他们过来吃晚餐。我把莱尼的所有痕迹都清除了。但当然，还是有线索，一本意大利游艇的精美宣传册，茶几中间的雪松木雪茄盒，前门附近挂钩上的一件巴宝莉男式大衣。我做了一大桌子菜，屋子里充满了家常的味道——大蒜、罗勒、香菜。那是个冬天，窗台上积满了雪。后院的聚光灯照在景观花园、红木桌、阿迪朗达克椅，以及去年春天死掉的天竺葵的巨大陶罐上。我在音响系统上播放着我父亲最爱的音乐，德沃夏克的《新世界交响曲》。

00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:40.020
<v Dani Shapiro>[MUSIC PLAYING - "SYMPHONY  NO. 9" BY ANTONIN DVORAK]
<v Dani Shapiro>[音乐播放 - 安东宁·德沃夏克的《第九交响曲》]

00:45:40.020 --> 00:46:19.710
<v Dani Shapiro>My parents rang the doorbell. They looked so solid standing on my front stoop, their cold, red noses poking out from above their mufflers. If nothing else, they looked like they belonged together. They were elegant and rangy, similarly proportioned, unlike Lenny and me. Lenny is thick as a linebacker, and I had become so delicate, the wind could have picked me up and blown me away. My mother strode into the brownstone as if it wasn't the weirdest thing in the world to be visiting her daughter in a lavish apartment with no name on the outside buzzer. My father trailed behind her wearily, as if setting foot on another planet.
<v Dani Shapiro>我父母按了门铃。他们站在我家门廊上看起来非常敦实，冰冷红润的鼻尖从围巾上方探出来。至少，他们看起来像是一对。他们优雅且修长，体型相称，不像莱尼和我。莱尼像个线卫一样壮实，而我变得如此脆弱，风一吹就能把我带走。母亲大步走进这栋褐石建筑，仿佛访问住在一个外面门铃上没写名字的豪华公寓里的女儿并不是世界上最怪异的事情。父亲疲惫地跟在她身后，仿佛踏上了另一个星球。

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:33.970
<v Dani Shapiro>My mother entered the living room, flung her arms wide, and did an impromptu dance to Dvorak. "Tra-la-la-la," she trilled. My father and I hung back and watched, our faces crumpled into awkward smiles.
<v Dani Shapiro>母亲走进客厅，张开双臂，随着德沃夏克的音乐跳了一段即兴舞蹈。“啦啦啦，”她轻哼道。父亲和我退后看着，我们的脸扭曲成尴尬的笑容。

00:46:33.970 --> 00:46:58.960
<v Dani Shapiro>We were used to it. In every family, there is room for only one Sarah Bernhardt, and my mother had assumed that role. It didn't occur to me that she was frightened, that this was a lot for her to take in, her college dropout daughter living in the lap of luxury. All I could see was her outsized self twirling around my living room in her fur coat and boots. I wanted a drink.
<v Dani Shapiro>我们习惯了。在每个家庭里，只能容纳一个莎拉·贝尔纳，而我母亲承担了这个角色。我没意识到她是害怕的，这对她来说实在难以消化，她那个辍学的女儿住在这个极其奢华的地方。我眼里只有她那庞大的身影，穿着皮草大衣和靴子在我客厅里旋转。我想喝酒。

00:46:58.960 --> 00:47:19.690
<v Dani Shapiro>I walked over to my mother and put a hand on her shoulder, and she spun to a halt. I took her coat and my father's and hung them above Lenny's raincoat by the front door. For the first time, I noticed there was a wreath made of twigs, a bit of Americana, on the wall near the kitchen. And I wondered if I could remove it quickly before my father saw it.
<v Dani Shapiro>我走向母亲，把手搭在她肩上，她转动着停了下来。我拿过她和父亲的大衣，挂在门边莱尼的雨衣上方。我第一次注意到厨房附近的墙上有一个用树枝做的花环，一种美式的小装饰。我在想能不能在父亲看到之前迅速把它取下来。

00:47:19.690 --> 00:47:48.820
<v Dani Shapiro>Wreaths under any circumstances are as goyishe as it gets. Which would be worse for my father, imagining that I was with some powerful guy old enough to be my father, or the possibility that the guy wasn't Jewish? I wished I could reassure him, yes Daddy, he's Jewish. 23 years older than me, a pathological liar, married to a woman who knows nothing of me, and a Jew.
<v Dani Shapiro>在任何情况下，花环都带有极其浓郁的非犹太风味。对父亲来说，哪种情况更糟呢？想象我和一个大到足以做我父亲的强力男人在一起，还是这个男人根本不是犹太人的可能性？我希望我可以让他放心，是的爸爸，他是犹太人。比我大23岁，一个病态的谎言家，娶了一个对我一无所知的女人的犹太人。

00:47:48.820 --> 00:48:11.870
<v Dani Shapiro>I poured myself two glasses of chardonnay for my parents and a large vodka for myself. I figured that if the vodka was in a water glass, they wouldn't know the difference, especially if I drank it like it was water. My drinking had taken on a new urgency in the past few months. It was no longer a question of desire, but of need. I could not get through an evening like this without the armor of booze.
<v Dani Shapiro>我为父母倒了两杯霞多丽，为自己倒了一大杯伏特加。我想如果伏特加倒在水杯里，他们就不会看出区别，尤其是如果我像喝水一样喝它的话。过去几个月里，我的酗酒有了一种新的紧迫感。那不再是一个愿望的问题，而是一个需求的问题。如果没有酒作为盔甲，我无法度过这样的夜晚。

00:48:11.870 --> 00:48:42.890
<v Dani Shapiro>I handed them their wine and directed them to the couch. On the coffee table, I had put out a plate of crudites and a bowl of olives. "Quite a place," my mother said brightly, her gaze darting around the room at the white brick fireplace with its wrought iron tools, the glass wall overlooking the garden, the soaring ceiling. My father stared at the fringe of the rug, glassy-eyed. He needed to be as numbed as I did to get through this night. "Thanks," I murmured, as if she was paying me a compliment.
<v Dani Shapiro>我递给他们葡萄酒，示意他们坐在沙发上。茶几上，我放了一盘蔬菜拼盘和一碗橄榄。“真是个好地方，”母亲轻快地说，目光在屋里扫视，看向带铁制工具的白砖壁炉、俯瞰花园的玻璃墙、高耸的天花板。父亲神情呆滞地盯着地毯的边缘。为了度过这个夜晚，他也需要像我一样麻木。我低声说了声“谢谢”，仿佛她是在赞美我。

00:48:42.890 --> 00:49:00.900
<v Dani Shapiro>I checked on dinner, using the opportunity to gulp some wine from the open bottle in the fridge. Vodka and white wine was a combination I knew worked for me. If I stuck with the formula, things shouldn't be too bad in the morning, especially if I wasn't eating. And I couldn't see myself eating.
<v Dani Shapiro>我检查了一下晚餐，趁机从冰箱里开着的酒瓶里猛喝了几口酒。伏特加加白葡萄酒是我知道对我有效的组合。如果我坚持这个配方，明早应该不会太糟糕，尤其是如果我不吃东西的话。而且我没法想象自己能吃下去。

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:22.830
<v Dani Shapiro>The music had stopped by the time we all sat at the dining room table, but I didn't notice then. If I had, I would certainly have changed the tape, filled the air with something other than the tinny, lonely sound of our three forks scraping against plates. I pushed my chicken from one side of my plate to the other. My stomach clenched and growled in protest.
<v Dani Shapiro>当我们坐在餐桌旁时，音乐已经停了，但我当时没注意。如果注意到了，我一定会换一盘磁带，让空气中充满除了我们三把叉子刮擦盘子的那种刺耳、孤独声之外的东西。我把鸡肉从盘子的一边推到另一边。我的胃紧缩着，发出抗议的咕噜声。

00:49:22.830 --> 00:49:36.540
<v Dani Shapiro>It seems that my parents and I, after 22 years in each other's company, had run out of things to say. I already knew their views on the political situation in Israel. And we couldn't discuss my school work. I was no longer in school.
<v Dani Shapiro>似乎我的父母和我，在彼此陪伴22年之后，已经无话可说了。我已经知道他们对以色列政治局势的看法。而且我们不能讨论我的学业。我已不在学校读书了。

00:49:36.540 --> 00:49:57.640
<v Dani Shapiro>My father pressed a corner of his napkin to his lips and murmured something about the food being delicious. My mother agreed. "My wonderful daughter," she said, shaking her head, "You've turned into such a little homemaker." I looked at my parents across the table. Is that what they really thought? How could they just sit there?
<v Dani Shapiro>父亲用餐巾纸的一角按了按嘴唇，低声说了句食物很美味。母亲表示赞同。“我出色的女儿，”她摇着头说，“你已经变成了一个如此精明的小管家。”我看着桌子对面的父母。他们真的这么想吗？他们怎么能就那么坐着？

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:22.150
<v Dani Shapiro>Some small piece of me wanted my father to fling me over his shoulder and carry me, kicking and screaming, to the car he had parked outside. I secretly wished that they would drive me home, deposit me in my childhood bedroom, and feed me chicken soup and saltines. I wanted to start my life over again, but I didn't know how.
<v Dani Shapiro>我内心深处有一小部分希望父亲能把我扛在肩上，带着踢踢打打、尖叫的我走到他停在外面的车上。我私下希望他们能带我回家，把我安置在儿时的卧室里，喂我喝鸡汤和吃饼干。我想重新开始我的生活，但我不知道该怎么做。

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:52.730
<v Dani Shapiro>In the face of the most tangible proof that Lenny had been lying to me all these years, I remained with him. "My little girl is dying," he would say whenever I noticed the discrepancies in his stories, or, "My children's mother is having electroshock therapy." When I couldn't take my own confusion anymore-- was Lenny lying to me? Was I going crazy? I decided to hire a detective to get to the bottom of it. By this time, my parents knew all about me and Lenny in theory, but it wasn't something we could talk about.
<v Dani Shapiro>面对莱尼多年来一直在对我撒谎的最实质性证明，我还是留在了他身边。“我的小女孩快死了，”每当我注意到他故事里的矛盾时，他就会这样说，或者说，“我孩子的母亲正在接受电击治疗。”当我自己实在无法承受这种困惑时——莱尼在对我撒谎吗？我疯了吗？我决定雇一个侦探来查个水落石出。到这时，我的父母在理论上都知道关于我和莱尼的一切，但这不是我们能谈论的话题。

00:50:52.730 --> 00:51:32.340
<v Dani Shapiro>When I think back to my younger self rifling through the New York City Yellow Pages in search of a private investigator, I feel like I'm watching a movie about someone else, a girl so clueless, she really didn't know that her desire to hire a detective was all the answer she needed. I chose a detective agency based on nothing more than its good address in the east 60s, a neighborhood filled with private schools and shrinks. "This isn't what you think," I told the detective. "I'm in a relationship with a married man, and I want you to find out if my boyfriend is cheating on me with his wife." At this, his eyebrows shot up. "Come again?"
<v Dani Shapiro>每当我回想起年轻的自己翻遍纽约黄页寻找私家侦探时，我感觉像在看一部关于别人的电影，那个女孩太迟钝了，她真不知道她想雇侦探这一行为本身就是她需要的所有答案。我选择那家侦探社仅仅是因为它在东60街有个好地址，那是个挤满了私立学校和心理医生的街区。“这不像你想的那样，”我告诉侦探。“我和一个已婚男人在交往，我想让你查查我男友是不是在和他的妻子偷情。”听到这话，他眉毛挑得老高。“再说一遍？”

00:51:32.340 --> 00:52:06.850
<v Dani Shapiro>"He claims his wife is in a mental hospital. He told me he hasn't been with her in years." "And you think he might be lying," said the investigator. Did I see the laughter behind his eyes, or is my memory supplying it now? Because I simply cannot imagine a middle-aged man listening to an earnest, overdressed 22-year-old girl tell him that she thinks her boyfriend might still be sleeping with his wife. "Yes," I said.
<v Dani Shapiro>“他声称他的妻子在精神病院。他告诉我他多年没跟她在一起了。”“而你认为他可能在撒谎，”调查员说。我是看到了他眼中的笑意，还是我的记忆在添油加醋？因为我简直无法想象一个中年男人听着一个一本正经、穿戴过度的22岁女孩告诉他，她认为她的男友可能还在和妻子睡觉时的情景。“是的，”我说。

00:52:06.850 --> 00:52:36.600
<v Dani Shapiro>Days later, I got the proof about Lenny's lies. In tears, I called my mother. "Oh, darling, I'm so sorry. Is there anything I can do?" "I don't think so." A pause. "Do you want me to call his wife?" My mother and Mrs. Klein had met each other at a few school functions back when none of this could have struck anyone as a remote possibility. "Yes," I said, "Call her." "I'll do it right now," my mother said.
<v Dani Shapiro>几天后，我得到了关于莱尼谎言的证据。我含着泪给母亲打电话。“噢，亲爱的，我很难过。有什么我能做的吗？”“我想没有。”沉默片刻。“你想让我给他妻子打电话吗？”母亲和克莱恩太太曾在几次学校活动上见过面，那时还没人觉得这一切有发生的可能。“是的，”我说，“打电话给她。”“我现在就打，”母亲说。

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:51.950
<v Dani Shapiro>I sat by the phone and watched the minutes tick by. I pictured Lenny's wife answering the phone with a chirpy hello, and my mother's slow, steady explanation of why she was calling. I had set in motion a chain of events which was now unstoppable.
<v Dani Shapiro>我坐在电话机旁，看着分分秒秒过去。我脑海中浮现出莱尼妻子接电话时那轻快的喂声，以及母亲缓慢、坚定地解释她为什么打电话。我启动了一连串现在已无法停止的事件。

00:52:51.950 --> 00:53:19.210
<v Dani Shapiro>More than 20 minutes passed before my mother called me back. "Well, I did it," she said. "You talked to her?" The world felt unreal, hallucinatory. "Yes. She called me a liar. She told me she has a happy marriage to a man who travels a lot, that he's on his way to California. And I said, 'No, he's on his way to see my daughter.'" My mother sounded proud of herself, immersed in the drama of the moment.
<v Dani Shapiro>过了20多分钟，母亲才回电给我。“嗯，我做了，”她说。“你和她谈过了？”世界感觉变得不真实，产生幻觉了。“是的。她骂我是个骗子。她告诉我她和一位经常出差的丈夫有着幸福的婚姻，他正在去加州的路上。我说，‘不，他正在去看我女儿的路上。’”母亲听起来对自己很满意，沉浸在当下的戏剧感中。

00:53:19.210 --> 00:53:30.920
<v Dani Shapiro>"How did she seem?" I asked. "What do you mean?" "Lenny's wife, was she angry?" "No," my mother said slowly. "She just didn't believe me, Dani."
<v Dani Shapiro>“她看起来怎么样？”我问。“你什么意思？”“莱尼的妻子，她生气了吗？”“不，”母亲缓缓地说。“她只是不相信我，丹妮。”

00:53:30.920 --> 00:53:45.680
<v Dani Shapiro>I spent the rest of that day in a state of awful excitement. Something was going to happen. And when Lenny showed up that evening at the apartment we were still sharing in the West Village, I was ready. He put his bags down and gave me a hug.
<v Dani Shapiro>那一天剩下的时间我都在一种可怕的兴奋中度过。一定会发生点什么的。当莱尼那天晚上出现在我们在西村共享的公寓里时，我准备好了。他放下包，拥抱了我一下。

00:53:45.680 --> 00:54:05.480
<v Dani Shapiro>The phone rang. My mother had given Mrs. Klein the number at the apartment and suggested she find out for herself what her husband was up to. Lenny picked up the phone on the kitchen wall. "Hello?" I watched him. And for the first and only time in the years I knew him, he looked genuinely surprised.
<v Dani Shapiro>电话响了。母亲给了克莱恩太太公寓的电话号码，并建议她自己去查查她丈夫在搞什么鬼。莱尼拿起厨房墙上的电话。“喂？”我看着他。这是我认识他的这些年来，第一次也是唯一一次，他看起来真的很惊讶。

00:54:05.480 --> 00:54:36.550
<v Dani Shapiro>He didn't say a word. He just listened for a few minutes, then hung up the phone. "That was my wife," he said. I was silent. "How did she get this number?" I shrugged. "I have to go." "I'd imagine," I said faintly. My anger was giving me the fuel that I needed to stay strong, at least for the moment.
<v Dani Shapiro>他没说一句话。他只是听了几分钟，然后挂断了电话。“那是我妻子，”他说。我沉默不语。“她是怎么得到这个号码的？”我耸了耸肩。“我得走了。”“我猜也是，”我虚弱地说。愤怒给了我保持坚强所需的燃料，至少在这一刻。

00:54:36.550 --> 00:55:08.760
<v Dani Shapiro>When Lenny slammed out of the apartment, I was certain I would never seen him again. I knew the truth now. It was staring me in the face, in the concrete form of flight lists and photos. And he knew that I knew. And besides, the whistle was blown. What could he possibly tell his wife? This was it, I told myself, absolutely, positively, the end.
<v Dani Shapiro>当莱尼摔门离开公寓时，我确信我再也不会见到他了。我现在知道了真相。它正以航班列表和照片的具体形式呈现在我面前。他也知道我知情了。而且，泄密了。他还能对他妻子说什么？我对自己说，这就是终点，绝对的、肯定的终点。

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:29.350
<v Dani Shapiro>It wasn't the end. Lenny still called 10, 12 times a day. He left messages on my answering machine. "Hello?" His voice filled my bedroom. "Fox, are you there?" Sometimes he didn't say a word. He would stay on the line for as long as five minutes, just breathing.
<v Dani Shapiro>这不是终点。莱尼仍然一天打10次，12次电话。他在我的答录机上留言。“喂？”他的声音填满了我的卧室。“福克斯，你在那吗？”有时他一言不发。他会在电话线上待上长达五分钟，只是呼吸。

00:55:29.350 --> 00:55:49.840
<v Dani Shapiro>Eventually, he did get to me again. And for the next year that we were together, three days here, four days there, my life became unrecognizable to me. I idly wondered what it would take to get me to leave him. I wondered about this over bottles of chilled white wine or heavy glasses half-filled with scotch.
<v Dani Shapiro>最终，他确实又让我回心转意了。在接下来的那一年里，我们在一起，待三天，四天，我的生活变得连我自己都不认识了。我无所事事地想，需要付出什么代价才能让我离开他。我看着冰镇的白葡萄酒或半满的烈性苏格兰威士忌酒杯思考着这个问题。

00:55:49.840 --> 00:56:08.280
<v Dani Shapiro>I was still wondering about it when I went to stay for a while at a health spa in California. The phone rang in my room one day. There had been a car crash on a snowy highway. My mother had 80 broken bones. My father was in a coma. They were lying in a hospital 3,000 miles away.
<v Dani Shapiro>当我去加州的一家健康水疗中心暂住时，我依然在思索着这个问题。有一天，房间里的电话响了。一条雪路发生车祸。母亲有80处骨折。父亲陷入了昏迷。他们躺在3000英里外的一家医院里。

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:41.880
<v Dani Shapiro>And suddenly, in ways I could not have imagined seconds earlier, nothing else mattered. As I packed my bags, I remembered my mother twirling, dancing to Dvorak, through the doors of Lenny's brownstone, and the glassy look in my father's eyes. I prayed that my father wouldn't die disappointed in me. And I knew then what I had to do.
<v Dani Shapiro>突然间，以我几秒钟前无法想象的方式，其他一切都不重要了。当我收拾行李时，我想起母亲旋转着、随着德沃夏克的音乐跳舞，穿过莱尼褐石公寓大门的场景，以及父亲眼中那种呆滞的神情。我祈祷父亲不会死在对我失望中。我知道那时我该做什么。

00:56:41.880 --> 00:57:00.730
<v Ira Glass>Dani Shapiro, reading an excerpt from he memoir, Slow Motion: A True Story, published by Random House.
<v Ira Glass>丹妮·夏皮罗，朗读其回忆录《慢动作：真实故事》，由兰登书屋出版。

